###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# Table I: on 48 CPU cores (936 atoms) MACE 0.90, ACE 33.84 and tabGAP
# 53.24 ns/day; on four A100 GPUs MACE 0.56 ns/day for 7,488 atoms, ACE 0.09
# and tabGAP 0.46 ns/day for 3,833,856 atoms. Per-atom times are 86,400 s /
# (ns/day x 1e6 steps) / atoms, i.e. wall time on the whole CPU set or GPU
# node. No training times are given.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - Fig. 3 prints energy RMSEs (0.0092, 0.0059, 0.0035, 0.0037) without a unit; they are recorded with unit null. The assignment of the two RMSE lines per panel to the models follows the force ranking stated in Sec. III A (MACE best, then ACE, GAP, tabGAP) and the statement that GAP and tabGAP have the lowest energy errors.
# - tabGAP is a new registry method; the paper describes it as GAP descriptor tabulation introduced by Glielmo et al. and extended by Byggmastar et al. (derivation from GAP not recorded here). The YBCO tabGAP is a tabulation of the YBCO GAP (App. F); tabulation is neither fine-tuning nor distillation, so no base model is recorded.
# - ACE body order (up to seven-body terms) and the GAP theta/delta hyperparameters have no hyperparameter individual in this encoding.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: datacite).
# - Evaluation roles and data, base models, inference timings with their system sizes, and the source location of each value are recorded in the encoding input (artifacts/kg/encoding/specs/eugenio2025.yaml) until the schema has terms for them.

